  "text": "**Review of *The Great Gatsby*: A Timeless Reflection on Dreams and Decadence**\n\nF. Scott Fitzgerald\u2019s *The Great Gatsby* is a masterful exploration of the American Dream, set against the backdrop of 1920s New York. The novel, which is a cornerstone of American literature, is known for its rich symbolism, complex characters, and lyrical prose.\n\nAt the heart of the story is Jay Gatsby, a mysterious millionaire whose lavish parties and penchant for glamour mask a deeper yearning for the past\u2014specifically, for the love of Daisy Buchanan, the woman he lost years ago. Gatsby\u2019s obsession with recapturing a bygone era reflects the novel\u2019s central theme: the illusion of reinvention and the futility of chasing dreams.\n\nThe setting of the story is crucial. East Egg and West Egg, two fictional places on Long Island, represent the divide between old money and new money. The contrast between these locations highlights the social stratification and moral decay that underpin the Roaring Twenties.\n\nDaisy Buchanan, Gatsby\u2019s love interest, is a symbol of the American Dream itself\u2014accessible, alluring, and ultimately elusive. Her character is shaped by the expectations of the elite society in which she lives, and her choices reflect the superficiality and fragility of the dream she embodies.\n\nThe character of Jay Gatsby himself is a proper noun that encapsulates the novel\u2019s themes. He is not just a person but an idea\u2014a man who becomes a symbol of the American Dream and its inevitable disillusionment. His tragic fate underscores the novel\u2019s tragic tone, as his pursuit of the dream leads to his downfall.\n\nThe title of the novel, *The Great Gatsby*, is a proper noun that acts as both a character and a narrative force. It suggests grandeur, aspiration, and ultimately, irony. The \u201cGreat\u201d in Gatsby\u2019s name is a nod to the legend that surrounds him, as well as the hollowness of his accomplishments.\n\nOther proper nouns in the novel, such as Daisy\u2019s home, the green light at the end of Daisy\u2019s dock, and the parties hosted by Gatsby, all serve to reinforce the themes of illusion, desire, and the impossibility of recapturing the past.\n\nUltimately, *The Great Gatsby* is not just a story of love and loss, but a critique of the American Dream itself. Fitzgerald\u2019s novel remains relevant because it speaks to the eternal human desire to reach for something greater than ourselves, even as we realize that such dreams may never be fully realized.\n\nWhether you are a first-time reader or a longtime fan, *The Great Gatsby* is a journey worth taking. It is a proper noun that deserves to be remembered.",
